OUTCOME WITHOUT ARCHITECTURAL ADJUSTMENT:
  CRITICAL SYSTEM FAILURE: The Linux Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Killer triggers!
  Without swap, the kernel has zero eviction backing store.
  If badness heuristic kills the ML or Real-Time process, factory shuts down.

=======================================================================
  ARCHITECTURAL MITIGATION STRATEGIES FOR THE 4TH TASK
=======================================================================
Option 1: In-Memory Compressed Swap (zram with LZ4 / Zstandard):
  - Allocate 512 MB of physical RAM for zram virtual swap device.
  - Yields ~1280 MB of effective uncompressed memory at 2.5:1 compression.
  - Net memory expansion: +307 MB (Accommodates the 500 MB task without disk I/O)!

Option 2: Model Quantization & Pruning (FP32 -> INT8 TensorRT/TFLite):
  - The 1.5 GB defect detection model is quantized from FP32 (4 bytes) to INT8 (1 byte).
  - Reduced ML Model Footprint: 1,536 MB -> 384 MB (Saves 1,152 MB of RAM!).
  - New Total System Usage:
    128 (OS) + 128 (RT) + 384 (ML) + 64 (Log) + 500 (Task 4) = 1204 MB / 2048 MB.
    Physical RAM Utilization: 58.8% (592 MB Safety Headroom Remaining)!

System Limitation Identified:
  Because the edge device has no secondary flash swap disk, memory is strictly
  finite. A hard hardware constraint exists: memory leaks in best-effort
  tasks must be strictly policed with Linux cgroups (memory.max).
